            <ArticleTitle>Removal of a femoral sheath following PTCA in cardiac patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>651-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Patients with coronary heart disease may require a percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty, involving the use of a balloon catheter to dilate the arteries introduced via a sheath. It is now increasingly common for the removal of the sheath to be a nursing procedure. This paper reviews the literature, best practice, potential complications and post-removal nursing care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
